::CW_U05_L07_Q01::A writer chooses a portfolio piece because feedback helped improve its ending. What does this show?{
~A. Random selection.#Incorrect. The choice is tied to growth.
=B. A strong selection rationale.#Correct. It explains why the piece belongs in the portfolio.
~C. A reason to avoid revision.#Incorrect. Feedback led to revision.
~D. A format error.#Incorrect. The issue is selection rationale.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q02::Which should happen before final polishing?{
~A. Fix only spelling.#Incorrect. Polishing comes after larger revision.
~B. Write the final note before reading the draft.#Incorrect. The note should match the final piece.
~C. Submit the rough draft.#Incorrect. Rough drafts are not automatically portfolio-ready.
=D. Check purpose, feedback, structure, and format needs.#Correct. Larger revision decisions should come first.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q03::Which is the strongest revision evidence?{
=A. A before-and-after sentence showing how feedback led to clearer imagery.#Correct. This proves a meaningful change.
~B. A statement that the draft is typed.#Incorrect. Typing is not revision evidence.
~C. A random color choice.#Incorrect. This does not show revision.
~D. A note that no changes were made.#Incorrect. That is not revision evidence.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q04::A poem has strong imagery but confusing line breaks. Which portfolio preparation step fits best?{
~A. Ignore the line breaks.#Incorrect. Format affects reader experience.
~B. Delete the poem automatically.#Incorrect. The poem may be revised.
=C. Revise the line breaks so the format supports meaning and pacing.#Correct. This addresses format-specific quality.
~D. Add a summary of every image.#Incorrect. Overexplaining does not fix format.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q05::Which portfolio note sentence is weakest?{
=A. I chose this because I like it.#Correct. This is vague and does not explain readiness.
~B. I selected this because feedback helped me clarify the ending.#Incorrect. This gives a strong reason.
~C. I revised the final image so the reader could infer the speaker's choice.#Incorrect. This explains craft effect.
~D. The final version is ready because the format and conventions are checked.#Incorrect. This explains readiness.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q06::A story's dialogue is unclear because speakers change in one paragraph. What should be checked?{
~A. Whether every sentence can be deleted.#Incorrect. Deleting everything is not targeted.
~B. Whether the title is random.#Incorrect. The problem is dialogue format.
~C. Whether the story should ignore readers.#Incorrect. Reader clarity matters.
=D. Paragraph breaks and dialogue tags.#Correct. These help readers track speakers.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q07::Which statement best separates revision from polishing?{
~A. Revision only fixes spelling.#Incorrect. That is polishing.
=B. Revision improves meaning or reader effect; polishing checks final details.#Correct. This accurately separates the two steps.
~C. Polishing should happen before purpose is clear.#Incorrect. Purpose comes first.
~D. Revision means changing words randomly.#Incorrect. Revision is purposeful.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q08::What should a writer do if feedback conflicts with the piece's purpose?{
~A. Accept it automatically.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated.
~B. Reject all future feedback.#Incorrect. Other feedback may be useful.
=C. Consider it, then choose the revision that protects the intended effect.#Correct. This uses feedback thoughtfully.
~D. Remove the piece from the portfolio immediately.#Incorrect. A conflict does not automatically disqualify the piece.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q09::Which piece is most portfolio-ready?{
~A. A rough draft with no review.#Incorrect. It lacks readiness checks.
~B. A piece selected only because it is short.#Incorrect. Selection needs a stronger rationale.
~C. A typed draft with random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not prove readiness.
=D. A revised piece with feedback evidence, checked format, and a clear portfolio note.#Correct. This includes the major readiness components.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q10::Which question should guide final readiness?{
=A. Does the final version show purpose, revision, format control, and reader readiness?#Correct. This checks the full portfolio standard.
~B. Is it the first file in the folder?#Incorrect. File order does not prove readiness.
~C. Did I avoid all feedback?#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered.
~D. Is it longer than every other piece?#Incorrect. Length does not prove quality.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q11::A writer adds one sensory image after feedback that the setting felt empty. What type of change is this?{
~A. Random decoration.#Incorrect. The change responds to feedback.
=B. A meaningful revision.#Correct. It improves setting detail based on feedback.
~C. A reason to skip format checks.#Incorrect. Format still matters.
~D. A title only.#Incorrect. The change is within the piece.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q12::Which response best explains final polish?{
~A. Changing the whole purpose at the last minute.#Incorrect. That is not polish.
=B. Checking conventions, consistency, and formatting after major revision is complete.#Correct. Polish comes after larger revisions.
~C. Ignoring punctuation.#Incorrect. Conventions are part of polish.
~D. Selecting pieces randomly.#Incorrect. Selection is a separate step.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q13::Which portfolio preparation step should come first?{
=A. Select a piece with a reason.#Correct. Selection begins the preparation plan.
~B. Write a final note before choosing a piece.#Incorrect. The note depends on the selected piece.
~C. Submit before reading feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered.
~D. Delete all drafts.#Incorrect. Drafts may provide revision evidence.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q14::A portfolio note says, "Feedback showed my ending was sudden." What should come next?{
~A. A statement that no revision happened.#Incorrect. The note should explain the response.
~B. An unrelated font choice.#Incorrect. The issue is the ending.
~C. A personal attack on the reviewer.#Incorrect. The response should stay craft-focused.
=D. An explanation of what was revised and how the ending improved.#Correct. This completes the reflection.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q15::Why should the final format match the creative form?{
=A. Format affects how readers follow structure, speakers, pacing, and meaning.#Correct. Format is part of publication quality.
~B. Format never matters.#Incorrect. Format changes reader experience.
~C. Format replaces revision evidence.#Incorrect. Both are needed.
~D. Format should be random.#Incorrect. Format choices should be purposeful.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q16::Which action weakens portfolio readiness?{
~A. Checking conventions after revision.#Incorrect. This strengthens readiness.
=B. Ignoring a confusing ending because the spelling is correct.#Correct. Surface correctness does not solve meaning problems.
~C. Explaining a revision decision.#Incorrect. This strengthens the portfolio note.
~D. Checking genre format.#Incorrect. This supports publication quality.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q17::Which statement best shows author growth?{
~A. I did not revise anything.#Incorrect. This does not show growth.
~B. I selected the first thing I found.#Incorrect. This does not show intentional selection.
~C. I changed the title randomly.#Incorrect. Random change is not growth.
=D. I used feedback to replace direct explanation with an image that lets the reader infer emotion.#Correct. This shows craft growth.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q18::What is the purpose of a selection rationale?{
=A. To explain why the piece belongs in the portfolio.#Correct. It connects the choice to growth or readiness.
~B. To replace the creative piece.#Incorrect. It supports, not replaces, the piece.
~C. To list every typo.#Incorrect. That is not selection rationale.
~D. To avoid revision.#Incorrect. It should often reference revision.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q19::Which revision should come before an author note?{
=A. Clarify the unclear speaker in the final scene.#Correct. The piece should be revised before the note explains it.
~B. Write a note that hides the confusion.#Incorrect. A note should not cover unresolved problems.
~C. Submit immediately.#Incorrect. Readiness checks are still needed.
~D. Ignore reader confusion.#Incorrect. Reader understanding matters.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q20::A writer's portfolio note explains purpose, feedback, revision, and readiness. What does this demonstrate?{
~A. It demonstrates random selection.#Incorrect. The explanation is purposeful.
~B. It demonstrates no revision.#Incorrect. It names revision.
=C. It demonstrates portfolio readiness reasoning.#Correct. It explains why the piece is ready.
~D. It demonstrates that format does not matter.#Incorrect. Readiness includes format.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q21::Which answer best handles a draft with strong meaning but weak conventions?{
=A. Preserve the meaning and complete a focused convention check before final submission.#Correct. This improves polish without damaging purpose.
~B. Delete the meaning.#Incorrect. The meaning is a strength.
~C. Ignore conventions entirely.#Incorrect. Conventions affect readability.
~D. Change the genre randomly.#Incorrect. Genre change is not needed.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q22::What makes a before-and-after example useful?{
=A. It shows exactly what changed and why the revision improved the piece.#Correct. This is strong revision evidence.
~B. It hides all changes.#Incorrect. Evidence should be visible.
~C. It proves first drafts are always final.#Incorrect. It shows revision.
~D. It replaces the final piece.#Incorrect. It supports the final piece.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q23::Which final checkpoint belongs in Lesson 7?{
~A. Private information collection.#Incorrect. That is not part of portfolio readiness.
=B. A portfolio-ready plan with selection rationale, revision evidence, final checklist, and note.#Correct. This matches the lesson checkpoint.
~C. A random list of favorite colors.#Incorrect. That does not address writing readiness.
~D. A refusal to revise.#Incorrect. Revision is required.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q24::A piece is polished but the writer cannot explain why it belongs in the portfolio. What is missing?{
=A. A selection rationale.#Correct. The writer needs to justify inclusion.
~B. A random new draft.#Incorrect. The issue is explanation.
~C. More spelling errors.#Incorrect. Errors would weaken readiness.
~D. A missing title only.#Incorrect. The missing piece is rationale.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q25::Which response best summarizes Lesson 7 mastery?{
=A. I selected a piece intentionally, revised from evidence, checked format and conventions, and wrote a portfolio note explaining readiness.#Correct. This includes the core mastery components.
~B. I typed a draft and stopped.#Incorrect. That is not portfolio-ready.
~C. I accepted every suggestion automatically.#Incorrect. Feedback must be evaluated.
~D. I chose a piece with no reason.#Incorrect. Selection needs a rationale.
}
